refentry.refsect1:
&reftitle.description;
public arrayIo\Poll\Context::wait Time\Durationnulltimeout&null; intnullmaxEvents&null;
Blocks until at least one watched handle is ready or the timeout expires, and returns the watchers whose events were triggered.
Unless Io\Poll\Event::EdgeTriggered or Io\Poll\Event::OneShot is used, a watcher is returned by every call for as long as its condition holds, for example until the pending data has been read. Such a watcher for Io\Poll\Event::Read on a stream is also returned while unread data remains in the read buffer of the stream.
If the context has no active watcher, the call can return immediately instead of waiting, depending on the backend.

  &reftitle.examples;

   Io\Poll\Context::wait example


<?php
use Io\Poll\Context;
use Io\Poll\Event;
use Time\Duration;

[$reader, $writer] = stream_socket_pair(STREAM_PF_UNIX, STREAM_SOCK_STREAM, STREAM_IPPROTO_IP);

$context = new Context();
$context->add(new StreamPollHandle($reader), [Event::Read], 'reader');
$context->add(new StreamPollHandle($writer), [Event::Write], 'writer');

// Only the writing end is ready.
foreach ($context->wait(Duration::fromMilliseconds(100)) as $watcher) {
    echo $watcher->getData(), PHP_EOL;
}

fwrite($writer, 'ping');

// Both ends are ready, but at most one watcher is returned.
var_dump(count($context->wait(Duration::fromMilliseconds(100), 1)));


   &example.outputs;


writer
int(1)
